{"name":"Park Avenue","hex":"#465048","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"park","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An area of land set aside for environment preservation or recreation.","A tract of ground kept in its natural state, about or adjacent to a residence, such as for the preservation of game, for walking, riding, or the like."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To bring (something such as a vehicle) to a halt or store in a specified place.","To defer (a matter) until a later date."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/park"},{"term":"avenue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A broad street, especially one bordered by trees or, in cities laid out in a grid pattern, one that is on a particular side of the city or that runs in a particular direction.","A way or opening for entrance into a place; a passage by which a place may be reached; a way of approach or of exit."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/avenue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Park Avenue","blocks":[{"text":"Park Avenue is a boulevard in New York City that carries north and southbound traffic in the boroughs of Manhattan and the Bronx. For most of the road's length in Manhattan, it runs parallel to Madison Avenue to the west and Lexington Avenue to the east. Park Avenue's entire length was formerly called Fourth Avenue; the title still applies to the section between Cooper Square and 14th Street. The avenue is called Union Square East between 14th and 17th streets, and Park Avenue South between 17th and 32nd streets."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bb/Park_Avenue_01.jpg/330px-Park_Avenue_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Park_Avenue","wikidata":"Q109711"},{"title":"Park Avenue (Montreal)","blocks":[{"text":"Park Avenue is one of central Montreal's major north-south streets. It derives its name from Mount Royal Park, by which it runs. Between Mount Royal Avenue and Pine Avenue, the street separates the eastern side of the mountain park and the smaller Jeanne Mance Park."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d2/Le_Plateau-Mont-Royal_-_Avenue_du_Parc_2.jpg/330px-Le_Plateau-Mont-Royal_-_Avenue_du_Parc_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Park_Avenue_(Montreal)","wikidata":"Q2874182"},{"title":"Park","blocks":[{"text":"A park is an area of natural, semi-natural or planted space set aside for human enjoyment and recreation or for the protection of wildlife or natural habitats. Urban parks are green spaces set aside for recreation inside towns and cities. National parks and country parks are green spaces used for recreation in the countryside. State parks and provincial parks are administered by sub-national government states and agencies. Parks may consist of grassy areas, rocks, soil and trees, but may also contain buildings and other artifacts such as monuments, fountains or playground structures. Many parks have fields for playing sports such as baseball and football, and paved areas for games such as basketball. Many parks have trails for walking, biking and other activities. Some parks are built adjacent to bodies of water or watercourses and may comprise a beach or boat dock area. Urban parks often have benches for sitting and may contain picnic tables and barbecue grills."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d3/Halleyparknovember_b_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Halleyparknovember_b_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Park","wikidata":"Q22698"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/59/Park_Avenue_tunnel.jpg/250px-Park_Avenue_tunnel.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/59/Park_Avenue_tunnel.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/59/Park_Avenue_tunnel.jpg/250px-Park_Avenue_tunnel.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The railroad tunnel in 1941","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APark_Avenue_tunnel.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fc/HotelMarguery1918.jpg/500px-HotelMarguery1918.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fc/HotelMarguery1918.jpg/500px-HotelMarguery1918.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Old Hotel Marguery on Park Avenue, flattened in the 1950s to make way for the Union Carbide Building","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHotelMarguery1918.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/ParkAvenueAug2004.JPG/330px-ParkAvenueAug2004.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/ParkAvenueAug2004.JPG/330px-ParkAvenueAug2004.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Park Avenue on the Upper East Side","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AParkAvenueAug2004.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a5/Park_Ave_E_188th_St_-_Bronx.jpg/500px-Park_Ave_E_188th_St_-_Bronx.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a5/Park_Ave_E_188th_St_-_Bronx.jpg/1280px-Park_Ave_E_188th_St_-_Bronx.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a5/Park_Ave_E_188th_St_-_Bronx.jpg/500px-Park_Ave_E_188th_S